-- Reference migration for the relational lease lock (`TomSqlLeaseLock`).
--
-- Creates the `tom_lease` table: one row per contended resource. This file is
-- the canonical DDL and is kept in sync with `leaseTableDdl()` in
-- `lib/src/tomserver/scheduling/sql_scheduling_sql.dart`;
-- `test/scheduling_schema_test.dart` fails if the two drift apart.
--
-- The table belongs in the *same* database as `tom_job_run`. A lock and a queue
-- in different systems can each be healthy while the other is unreachable, and
-- the resulting disagreement — the queue says node A claimed the run, the lock
-- says node B holds it — has no resolution.
--
-- Two columns are load-bearing and neither is obvious:
--
--  * `token` is the fencing counter. A row is kept after release (with
--    `released = 1`) rather than deleted, because deleting it would restart the
--    counter at 1 for the next holder, and a token that repeats is not a
--    fencing token. Retaining the row is also what lets `inspect()` distinguish
--    an orderly departure from a holder that vanished.
--  * `version` is incremented on every write. MariaDB reports *changed* rows,
--    not matched rows, so an UPDATE writing the values already present affects
--    zero rows; a renewal landing in the same clock instant with the same TTL
--    would then look like a lost lease and fall through to takeover. `version`
--    makes a matching update always change the row, and doubles as the
--    compare-and-set guard that lets exactly one of several racing takeovers
--    win.
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS tom_lease (
  resource VARCHAR(191) NOT NULL,
  owner VARCHAR(191) NOT NULL,
  token BIGINT NOT NULL,
  acquired_at TIMESTAMP(6) NOT NULL,
  expires_at TIMESTAMP(6) NOT NULL,
  released SMALLINT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
  version BIGINT NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,
  PRIMARY KEY (resource)
);
